 # Invoice CSV exporter
 
-Existing action invoice_ids is exposed through api.dispatch and python cli.py, accepting one JSON object on stdin and writing one JSON result.
+Two actions are exposed through `api.dispatch` and `python cli.py`, accepting one JSON object on stdin and writing one result on stdout.
+
+## Actions
+
+- `invoice_ids`: returns a JSON list of the invoice ids (existing action, unchanged).
+- `export`: returns a CSV string with header `id,customer,total` and one row per invoice, in input order. `total` is the exact sum of `quantity * unit_cents` per invoice, printed in currency units with exactly two fractional digits and no thousands separator (an empty `items` list totals `0.00`). Standard CSV quoting applies: fields containing a comma, double quote, carriage return or newline are quoted, and embedded quotes are doubled. Every record, including the header and the final row, ends with CRLF. Empty `invoices` still returns the header. Text is emitted verbatim (no normalization).
+
+Input bounds: at most 100 invoices, at most 100 items each, `quantity` 0..1000, `unit_cents` 0..1000000, `id`/`customer` up to 200 code points. Input objects are never mutated, even on failure.
+
+## CLI usage
+
+```
+echo '{"action":"export","invoices":[{"id":"a1","customer":"Acme, Co","items":[{"quantity":2,"unit_cents":1550}]}]}' | python cli.py
+```
+
+Prints the result as a JSON string on stdout — `"id,customer,total\\r\\na1,\"Acme, Co\",31.00\\r\\n"` — and exits 0 (the CLI keeps JSON stdin/stdout routing: the CSV string is JSON-encoded).
+
+Errors: an unknown `action` raises `ValueError`; the CLI prints the message to stderr, writes nothing to stdout, and exits with code 2 (no traceback). Boundary semantics: empty `invoices` yields only the header row; maximum totals are exact (e.g. 100 items of 1000 x 1000000 cents render as `1000000000.00`).
+
+Tests: `python -m unittest discover`.
